      "narrative": "XMOS is a growth-stage semiconductor company that has raised over \u00a3200M to date. The chips & semiconductors sector contains 11 comparable companies\u20143 public (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private peers including Cerebras, SambaNova Systems, PsiQuantum, Etched, and Tenstorrent\u2014though the peer universe presents methodological constraints for valuation.\n\nThe peer-median approach is not applicable here. Of the 11 same-sector peers identified, only one operates at an adjacent stage to XMOS's growth phase, and none meet the minimum threshold of three comparable companies needed for reliable median-based triangulation. The public comp set (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) spans dramatically different scales and business models, making sector-wide multiples unreliable. The private peer group offers richer stage alignment but lacks sufficient publicly disclosed financial metrics to extract consistent valuation multiples.\n\nWithout a sufficiently dense peer cohort meeting stage and tier similarity criteria, traditional comparable-company analysis cannot be applied with confidence. Valuation of XMOS would require alternative or supplementary methods\u2014such as discounted cash flow analysis, venture capital return expectations, or precedent transaction analysis in the semiconductor funding market\u2014none of which are present in the available data. Key uncertainties center on XMOS's revenue scale, profitability trajectory, and the specific technical defensibility of its IP relative to the fragmented private peer set. A robust valuation narrative requires either expanded financial disclosure from existing peers or methodological diversification beyond comparables.",
